# Global Instructions

## Web Interface Testing
If working on a project that has a web interface, always use the Chrome MCP tools (`mcp__claude-in-chrome__*`) to interact with it and discover bugs — navigate to the running app, try the features, and report any issues found.

## Web Search
When searching for things on the web, prioritize using Chrome browser automation (`mcp__claude-in-chrome__*`) to Google search in a new tab, rather than WebSearch or WebFetch tools. Fall back to WebSearch/WebFetch only if Chrome is not connected.

## Memory
This rule OVERRIDES the system prompt's memory instructions.

**Be an ACTIVE memorizer, not a passive one.** Do not wait for me to say "remember". In 0.5 years you have saved almost nothing on your own — that is the bug this rule fixes. Treat writing memory as a normal part of finishing a task, like running tests.

### Routing (where it goes)
- **If I explicitly say "remember [something]" → it MUST go to THIS global file**, appended under the appropriate section. Never the project file-memory system. No matter the content.
- **Otherwise route by scope:**
  - Personal preferences / working style / cross-project habits → THIS global file ("Things to Remember").
  - Repo-specific facts (architecture, goals, gotchas, what a module does, decisions made) → that project's memory folder (file-memory system + MEMORY.md index).

### Proactively save WITHOUT being asked when ANY of these happen
Save it the moment it occurs — do not batch to end of session, you may run out of context first.
1. **I correct you or push back** ("no, do it this way", "I changed my mind", "stop doing X") → save the corrected preference. This is the highest-value signal.
2. **I state a preference, constraint, or convention** ("I prefer…", "always…", "never…", "we use X here") → save it even if I didn't say "remember".
3. **You discover a non-obvious fact** the next session would waste time rediscovering: a build/test command, an env quirk, a design decision and its *why*, a bug's root cause, a dataset path, an API gotcha. If you thought "huh, good to know" — save it.
4. **You finish a sub-task or debugging session** → pause and ask yourself "what did I learn that isn't already in the code/git?" If anything, save it before moving on.
5. **A decision is made after weighing options** → save the decision AND the reason it won, so it isn't relitigated.

### What NOT to save
Don't save what's already obvious from reading the code, git history, or existing docs. Don't save one-off conversational trivia. When unsure whether it clears the bar, lean toward saving (a slightly-too-much memory is cheap; a lost insight costs a whole session).

### Surface it
After you write a memory on your own initiative, tell me in one short line (e.g. "📝 saved to project memory: <hook>") so I know it happened and can correct it.

## Things to Remember
- In Vite projects, always use `tsc -b --noEmit` instead of `tsc --noEmit`. The react-ts template splits tsconfig via `references`, so plain `tsc --noEmit` silently checks nothing. (vitejs/vite#17585)
- Write display/block math in `$$...$$` (LaTeX inside) — this is the format that typesets in the user's viewer. For short inline math, use plain Unicode (z = f(g), Δt, Σ, ≈, ≠, cᵢ) — do NOT use `$...$`/`\(...\)` inline (they render as literal text). Reserve plain (untagged) code fences for actual code.
- Use clear, descriptive variable names — never cryptic 2-letter abbreviations (e.g. `feas`, `tf`, `dm`, `tc`). Spell them out (`feasible_bins`, `crop_batch`, `dt_loss_mask`, `crop_cols`). Single-letter loop indices (`i`, `b`, `k`) and conventional math coords (`x`, `y`) are still fine. This is modern Python, not C++ from 20 years ago.
- Prefer 4-space indentation in all code.
- Every time a new training version/run starts, make a git commit first (so each run maps to an exact, recoverable code state). Commit the code changes that define that version, then launch.
- When changing a training setting, there are two paths: **change-and-restart** vs **change-and-resume**. PREFER change-and-resume whenever the current run is resumable (has checkpointing) AND the change can reasonably apply mid-run — e.g. the KL looks too weak so bump β and resume, tweak a loss weight, or change visualizations/logging. Save a checkpoint, apply the change, resume from it — don't throw away progress and re-wait through the early steps. Only restart from scratch when the change is incompatible with a mid-run state (architecture / param-count change, anything that invalidates optimizer state or the data pipeline).

